The "gun" is just a 6-cell glider. Every 3 generations, it sheds a single "spark" cell. This spark temporarily splits the pattern into two connected components: the single spark cell, and the 6-cell glider itself. 

The `[REDACTED_SECRET]` script saw multiple components, checked if any of them was a glider, and saw the 6-cell glider. It counted this as an "emission." It then checked if the remaining "core" matched the initial pattern's canonical form. Since the "core" was just the 6-cell glider, its canonical shape *did* match the initial shape. The script completely failed to check that the core was actually translating across the board rather than staying put!

