### 2026-08-15T00:03:25Z | agy | agy | conversation 4abc6e66-08cd-4cb4-9377-f2da45e943a9

Ah! I got extremely excited for a moment because my reflector search outputted a hit, but it was a **false positive**.

The script falsely flagged it because gliders in this rule don't just translate rigidly—they actually flip their shape and bounding-box orientation over their 3-generation cycle (a glide reflection). The script compared the bounding box of the glider at step `N` to step `N+3`, saw that its dimensions flipped, and incorrectly concluded it had changed direction!

When I traced the "reflected" collision manually, I saw the glider was shot from so far away that it just harmlessly flew past the oscillator without touching it!

I've just corrected the geometry check. It now traces the true center of mass over a full 6-generation cycle to ensure it doesn't get tricked by the shape-flipping, and I'm re-running the reflector search.


